is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router1
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: BGP control plane on router1 (bgpd daemon and configuration).

Mechanism: With eBGP down on router1, no routes are exchanged between the two ASes. Each router only knows its directly connected subnets, so hosts can reach their local gateway but have no path to the partner network. This matches the report: pc1 can ping 195.11.14.1 (router1) while pc2 can ping 200.1.1.1 (router2), but inter-AS traffic fails.

Localization: On router1, “show ip bgp summary” returns “bgpd is not running,” and its running config contains no BGP stanza at all. Its routing table shows only connected routes: 193.10.11.0/24 (to router2) and 195.11.14.0/24 (to pc1). On router2, BGP is configured (AS 2) and the neighbor 193.10.11.1 (router1) is stuck in Active with 0 prefixes, and its routing table likewise contains only connected routes 193.10.11.0/24 and 200.1.1.0/24. This proves the interconnect is up at L3 but no BGP session exists.

Root cause: The BGP daemon and configuration on router1 (AS 1) are missing/not running, so the eBGP session to router2 never establishes.

Corrective fix: On router1, enable and start bgpd, and configure BGP for AS 1:
- router bgp 1
- neighbor 193.10.11.2 remote-as 2
- address-family ipv4 unicast
  network 195.11.14.0/24
After bgpd starts and the session establishes (Established state on both sides), prefixes for 195.11.14.0/24 and 200.1.1.0/24 will be exchanged and pc1↔pc2 reachability will be restored. Persist the config and ensure bgpd is enabled on boot. 
=== END ===